Save the player's name when it is entered, not only at exit.

Upstream calls `config_save` from exactly one place - `ball/main.c:602`, after the main
loop returns. On a desktop that is sound: quitting is how a game ends, so every setting
is written on the way out.

A console title does not end that way. It is closed from the dashboard or killed, `main`
never returns, and nothing is written. Most settings survive that anyway, because the
player reaches them through the options screen and some earlier session exited cleanly
once - but the name cannot, because `st_title` sends the player to this screen whenever
`CONFIG_PLAYER` is empty and that is the state every launch starts in. The name was
therefore the one setting that could never persist, and the port asked for it on every
single launch while `neverballrc` sat on disk beside it with `player` blank.

So it is written the moment it is entered. That costs one file write on a screen the
player reaches rarely, and it is the narrowest change that fixes it - the alternative,
saving on the close signal, races the system's own kill timer and would have to be right
about how long there is.

diff --git a/ball/st_name.c b/ball/st_name.c
index ed2f023..8ab4f11 100644
--- a/ball/st_name.c
+++ b/ball/st_name.c
@@ -71,6 +71,15 @@ static int name_action(int tok, int val)
 
         config_set_s(CONFIG_PLAYER, text_input);
 
+        /* **Written out here, not only at exit.** Upstream calls `config_save` in exactly one
+         * place - `ball/main.c:602`, after the main loop returns - which is fine on a desktop,
+         * where quitting is how a game ends. A console title is closed from the dashboard or
+         * killed, and then `main` never returns and nothing is saved; `st_title` sends the
+         * player to this screen whenever the name is empty, so the name was the one setting
+         * that could never survive, and it was asked for on every single launch. Saving the
+         * moment it is entered costs one file write on a screen the player reaches rarely. */
+        config_save();
+
         return goto_state(ok_state);
 
     case GUI_CL:
